WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.012 --> 00:00:06.781
[SOUND] It's my great
pleasure to have

00:00:06.782 --> 00:00:10.550
Rodrigo Castano from University
of Buenos Aires visiting us.

00:00:10.551 --> 00:00:12.200
He's a PhD student there, and

00:00:12.201 --> 00:00:15.620
working on making model checkers
more accessible to users.

00:00:15.621 --> 00:00:18.030
And look forward to the talk,
welcome.

00:00:18.031 --> 00:00:22.750
&gt;&gt; Right, thanks, thank you for
the introduction, Shivendu.

00:00:22.751 --> 00:00:23.600
And again,

00:00:23.601 --> 00:00:26.790
I'm gonna be talking about,
well, explaining inconclusive

00:00:26.791 --> 00:00:28.590
outcomes from software
model checkers to users.

00:00:28.591 --> 00:00:31.590
And this will mostly
cover my PhD work,

00:00:31.591 --> 00:00:34.440
and it'll extend a little bit
into the future, with stuff that

00:00:34.441 --> 00:00:38.330
I would look forward to do in
the next few months/years.

00:00:38.331 --> 00:00:40.836
So let's start with motivation.

00:00:40.837 --> 00:00:44.736
So why do I wanna care about
inconclusive model checker

00:00:44.737 --> 00:00:45.510
results?

00:00:45.511 --> 00:00:48.581
Well, because I might wanna
check any of the properties

00:00:48.582 --> 00:00:50.300
that you have on your right.

00:00:50.301 --> 00:00:53.772
And we have assertions in the
code, and we have assertions of

00:00:53.773 --> 00:00:56.370
invariance, and
it's just regular C code.

00:00:56.371 --> 00:00:57.904
We're not dealing with models,

00:00:57.905 --> 00:01:00.590
we're actually dealing
with source code.

00:01:00.591 --> 00:01:04.153
And I might just choose a model
checker that I like, I might

00:01:04.154 --> 00:01:07.789
choose a set time limit that
I have, maybe it's 1 minute,

00:01:07.790 --> 00:01:11.378
maybe it's 15 minutes,
half an hour, whatever time.

00:01:11.379 --> 00:01:15.018
I run my software model checker,
and then I come back, and

00:01:15.019 --> 00:01:17.544
I get unknown,
time limit exceeded, or

00:01:17.545 --> 00:01:21.557
maybe memory limit exceeded, or
maybe it had an obstruction and

00:01:21.558 --> 00:01:24.440
we find an error, or
any of those things.

00:01:24.441 --> 00:01:27.210
Or maybe I'm using a bounded
model checker, and I get, well,

00:01:27.211 --> 00:01:29.470
I didn't find a bug, but
I've reached the bound,

00:01:29.471 --> 00:01:31.290
so it's still an unsound result.

00:01:32.380 --> 00:01:34.530
So what do I get from this?

00:01:34.531 --> 00:01:36.670
This is not a really
useful result,

00:01:36.671 --> 00:01:39.000
because what do I do next?

00:01:39.001 --> 00:01:40.780
Do I extend the time of it?

00:01:40.781 --> 00:01:41.830
Do I trust the code?

00:01:41.831 --> 00:01:42.535
Can I trust the code?

00:01:42.536 --> 00:01:45.859
Can I just commit this code
that doesn't have any tests,

00:01:45.860 --> 00:01:47.390
and just use it like this?

00:01:47.391 --> 00:01:50.040
Should I change the technique?

00:01:50.041 --> 00:01:51.060
It's not really useful, right?

00:01:51.061 --> 00:01:52.770
So I feel like this is
a topic that actually

00:01:52.771 --> 00:01:54.180
requires attention.

00:01:54.181 --> 00:01:56.010
And I think it could
have an impact, and

00:01:56.011 --> 00:01:58.670
we're gonna talk about how
it could have an impact.

00:01:58.671 --> 00:02:00.110
So let's take a step back.

00:02:00.111 --> 00:02:05.345
So we have basically
two ways of seeing verifications/bug-finding,

00:02:05.346 --> 00:02:05.831
right?

00:02:05.831 --> 00:02:07.896
So we have bug-finding
techniques,

00:02:07.897 --> 00:02:11.639
such as bounding model checking,
fuzzing, [INAUDIBLE] execution,

00:02:11.640 --> 00:02:12.770
and all that.

00:02:12.771 --> 00:02:14.300
And basically, the workflow is,

00:02:14.301 --> 00:02:18.520
you test your system until
basically, you've tested enough,

00:02:18.521 --> 00:02:21.190
and you assume that your
system works, right?

00:02:21.191 --> 00:02:23.010
You put it in production,
it runs, and

00:02:23.011 --> 00:02:26.120
then, well, if it bugs,
it has bugs.

00:02:27.660 --> 00:02:30.292
But then you have the opposite,
sort of.

00:02:30.293 --> 00:02:32.960
And obviously,
usually the type of systems that

00:02:32.961 --> 00:02:36.020
are tackled by each of these
techniques are different,

00:02:36.021 --> 00:02:39.290
right, that's why you also
have different baselines.

00:02:39.291 --> 00:02:42.290
So if you're working with
assisted or automatic

00:02:42.291 --> 00:02:46.242
verification, but you're trying
to have full verification,

00:02:46.243 --> 00:02:49.974
maybe your baseline is, well,
my program might have bugs,

00:02:49.975 --> 00:02:53.579
unless I find full proof that
the program is safe, right?

00:02:53.580 --> 00:02:57.579
So what you do is, you attempt
verification, and maybe you

00:02:57.580 --> 00:03:01.765
timeout, or you get some
counterexample that's superior.

00:03:01.766 --> 00:03:03.210
And then you simplify
the property and

00:03:03.211 --> 00:03:05.430
then you simplify the program or
refine the abstraction.

00:03:05.431 --> 00:03:08.272
Essentially, you work on
the instance of verification,

00:03:08.273 --> 00:03:09.259
then you try again.

00:03:09.260 --> 00:03:12.427
And if you can't manage to
get a conclusive result,

00:03:12.428 --> 00:03:15.890
then you basically assume that
your program is not safe.

00:03:15.891 --> 00:03:17.507
You don't have any guarantees,

00:03:17.508 --> 00:03:20.289
you don't know how far your
model checker went, right?

00:03:20.290 --> 00:03:23.400
So both of these
approaches have, sorry.

00:03:25.000 --> 00:03:27.870
&gt;&gt; Are you talking about
user assisted verification?

00:03:27.871 --> 00:03:31.700
How does this fit in there?

00:03:31.701 --> 00:03:33.697
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so-
&gt;&gt; [INAUDIBLE]

00:03:33.698 --> 00:03:35.653
&gt;&gt; If you're working with, for

00:03:35.654 --> 00:03:39.642
instance, Dafny, right, and
you have to add invariants or

00:03:39.643 --> 00:03:43.180
whatever, and you get
a timeout and stuff like that.

00:03:43.181 --> 00:03:45.291
Well, you try and
simplify your properties, and

00:03:45.292 --> 00:03:47.559
maybe just look under the hood,
why is it timing out?

00:03:47.560 --> 00:03:49.625
And then you split
your properties, and

00:03:49.626 --> 00:03:52.960
then maybe you add invariants
and so on and so forth.

00:03:52.961 --> 00:03:56.278
But unless you have a full
proof, you won't say, okay,

00:03:56.279 --> 00:04:00.237
well, I've tried for maybe two
hours, this must be good, right?

00:04:00.238 --> 00:04:01.291
Usually you don't do that.

00:04:01.292 --> 00:04:04.520
That's not the workflow
that most people follow.

00:04:04.521 --> 00:04:06.569
&gt;&gt; You're saying
that the problem is,

00:04:06.570 --> 00:04:07.766
you have a black box.

00:04:07.767 --> 00:04:13.148
And [INAUDIBLE] you don't
have a good explanation for

00:04:13.149 --> 00:04:16.577
why, and so you're hacking it.

00:04:16.578 --> 00:04:19.048
You're like, maybe if I,

00:04:19.049 --> 00:04:22.630
is that the [INAUDIBLE]
you're describing?

00:04:22.631 --> 00:04:25.811
&gt;&gt; No, what I'm saying is that-
&gt;&gt; You're trying-

00:04:25.812 --> 00:04:27.059
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so what I'm saying is

00:04:27.060 --> 00:04:28.827
that usually if you're
an expert user,

00:04:28.828 --> 00:04:32.220
you will have some insight as
to what you're supposed to do.

00:04:32.221 --> 00:04:34.630
But unless you reach full proof,

00:04:34.631 --> 00:04:37.120
you won't assume that
your program is safe.

00:04:37.121 --> 00:04:40.160
You won't get halfway through
the proof and be like, I mean,

00:04:40.161 --> 00:04:41.480
I'm pretty sure this works.

00:04:41.481 --> 00:04:43.315
Usually when you're doing that,

00:04:43.316 --> 00:04:46.300
you won't assume your
program works, right, sorry.

00:04:46.301 --> 00:04:50.297
&gt;&gt; If you're using Dafny, then
the expectation, I think, is you

00:04:50.298 --> 00:04:54.260
have a property and a program,
and you're trying to prove.

00:04:54.261 --> 00:04:56.786
&gt;&gt; That's right.
&gt;&gt; Right, so if it's timing out,

00:04:56.787 --> 00:05:01.970
the user assumption is, I
haven't gotten it approved yet.

00:05:01.971 --> 00:05:02.601
&gt;&gt; That's right, yeah.

00:05:02.602 --> 00:05:05.500
&gt;&gt; But I don't know, I might
have all the information.

00:05:06.500 --> 00:05:09.311
All the information might be
present, it's just the theorem

00:05:09.312 --> 00:05:11.373
prover's not good enough
to do it right now.

00:05:11.374 --> 00:05:12.666
I have to do something.

00:05:12.667 --> 00:05:13.731
&gt;&gt; That's right, so

00:05:13.732 --> 00:05:17.200
what would you assume about the
system is sort of my question?

00:05:17.201 --> 00:05:19.580
Would you assume that
the program is safe to use?

00:05:19.581 --> 00:05:21.660
Would you release it into
production without a full proof?

00:05:21.661 --> 00:05:24.683
Do you have any notion
of how far you've got?

00:05:24.684 --> 00:05:25.630
Yes, sir.

00:05:25.631 --> 00:05:26.990
&gt;&gt; So what about the case where

00:05:28.390 --> 00:05:31.030
there's a property that I know
holds as a programmer, right?

00:05:31.031 --> 00:05:33.819
I know that property holds, and
I can't prove that property.

00:05:33.820 --> 00:05:36.988
Now if you could just assert
the property is true, I mean,

00:05:36.989 --> 00:05:38.994
that way the proof
would go through,

00:05:38.995 --> 00:05:41.841
assuming that it doesn't
timeout for some reason,

00:05:41.842 --> 00:05:44.710
which that's a whole
different category of issue.

00:05:44.711 --> 00:05:48.445
But the point is, I think
the human can get a little bit

00:05:48.446 --> 00:05:52.263
of sense that he can help
the theorem prover by giving it

00:05:52.264 --> 00:05:55.915
sort of extra information
that the program has, but

00:05:55.916 --> 00:05:58.630
the verification system doesn't.

00:05:58.631 --> 00:06:01.130
&gt;&gt; That's what he means by
simplify the property, so

00:06:01.131 --> 00:06:04.590
we're adding an assumption that
you know would be simplified.

00:06:04.591 --> 00:06:07.405
&gt;&gt; Yeah, okay, but at that
point then, why would you stop,

00:06:07.406 --> 00:06:08.160
in some sense?

00:06:08.161 --> 00:06:11.970
Cuz you can always give
it enough information?

00:06:11.971 --> 00:06:14.711
&gt;&gt; Yeah, but the question is, at
some point, maybe you think that

00:06:14.712 --> 00:06:16.390
a property holds, but
you're not absolutely sure.

00:06:16.391 --> 00:06:18.084
I mean, if you know
that a property holds,

00:06:18.085 --> 00:06:19.790
you definitely add it
to your assumption.

00:06:19.791 --> 00:06:22.630
I mean, and then you have a
conclusive verification result.

00:06:22.631 --> 00:06:24.440
But if you don't
reach that point,

00:06:24.441 --> 00:06:26.180
if you keep iterating on this,
and you're like,

00:06:26.181 --> 00:06:29.140
okay, I'm adding assumptions
that I'm not really sure hold

00:06:29.141 --> 00:06:31.640
right now because I don't really
know what this lever does, or

00:06:31.641 --> 00:06:32.900
this other thing.

00:06:32.901 --> 00:06:36.179
Then maybe you wanna know, well,
are my assumptions too strong?

00:06:36.180 --> 00:06:39.070
When does this assumption
actually affect me?

00:06:39.071 --> 00:06:44.510
Is this relevant to my system or
not, right?

00:06:44.511 --> 00:06:47.125
So problems, well, bug finding
and testing doesn't really

00:06:47.126 --> 00:06:49.300
have that many guarantees,
cuz you're testing.

00:06:49.301 --> 00:06:50.885
So if you didn't find a bug,

00:06:50.886 --> 00:06:54.070
you don't know whether
there exists one or not.

00:06:54.071 --> 00:06:56.594
&gt;&gt; So it has a guarantee
if you find one.

00:06:56.595 --> 00:06:58.276
&gt;&gt; Well,
if you've found one, yeah,

00:06:58.277 --> 00:07:01.275
but if you haven't found one,
you don't have many guarantees.

00:07:01.276 --> 00:07:03.848
And the problem with the other
approach is that, well,

00:07:03.849 --> 00:07:05.230
it's kind of all or nothing.

00:07:05.231 --> 00:07:08.080
If you follow that idea,
that paradigm of, okay,

00:07:08.081 --> 00:07:10.870
if I don't have a full proof,
then I'm now gonna give up,

00:07:10.871 --> 00:07:12.400
then it's kind of all or
nothing, right?

00:07:12.401 --> 00:07:14.480
If you didn't manage to
make the whole proof,

00:07:14.481 --> 00:07:16.790
all of the effort that you
spent on it Is wasted.

00:07:16.791 --> 00:07:19.655
And you have to start over with
whatever other technique you're

00:07:19.656 --> 00:07:20.192
gonna use.

00:07:20.193 --> 00:07:23.102
And then, to be honest,
there's not that many people

00:07:23.103 --> 00:07:26.250
using these sort of techniques,
right, in industry.

00:07:26.251 --> 00:07:29.741
Basically, testing is way
more widespread than formal

00:07:29.742 --> 00:07:31.524
verification techniques,

00:07:31.525 --> 00:07:34.432
in the sense of trying
to achieve a full proof.

00:07:34.433 --> 00:07:38.648
So what if we take a different
perspective, right, and we use

00:07:38.649 --> 00:07:42.540
these sort of verification
tools, but we use them in a way

00:07:42.541 --> 00:07:46.378
as, okay, this could be
better than testing, right?

00:07:46.379 --> 00:07:48.780
But I wanna know how
good it was, right?

00:07:48.781 --> 00:07:52.370
And let's assume that Visual
Studio checked every assertion,

00:07:52.371 --> 00:07:53.240
memory safety, and

00:07:53.241 --> 00:07:55.310
I'm doing a little bit of
science fiction here, right?

00:07:55.311 --> 00:07:57.661
But it checks all of
these properties,

00:07:57.662 --> 00:08:00.654
system API is [INAUDIBLE],
then we have a dashboard

00:08:00.655 --> 00:08:04.330
showing us how good the
verification attempt was, right?

00:08:04.331 --> 00:08:06.840
So we have maybe on the left
the things that were probably

00:08:06.841 --> 00:08:10.310
checked, that we got
a complete safe result, and

00:08:10.311 --> 00:08:11.610
we have the proof that it holds.

00:08:11.611 --> 00:08:13.560
And we have on the right
maybe the warnings,

00:08:13.561 --> 00:08:15.681
and we could have different
kinds of warnings.

00:08:15.682 --> 00:08:18.853
We could have warnings that
might be false positives, and

00:08:18.854 --> 00:08:21.637
then they can be ranked
according to how likely that

00:08:21.638 --> 00:08:25.001
it's a false positive, or Or
that it's a true positive, and

00:08:25.002 --> 00:08:27.783
then in the middle,
we have a sort of an explanation

00:08:27.784 --> 00:08:30.550
of what your model checker did,
right?

00:08:30.551 --> 00:08:33.390
What sort of behaviors did it
cover, what part of the code did

00:08:33.391 --> 00:08:35.980
it cover, do you have any
code coverage things?

00:08:35.981 --> 00:08:36.960
And maybe,

00:08:36.961 --> 00:08:39.740
suppose this is all integrated
into the code review process.

00:08:39.741 --> 00:08:42.850
And instead of asking
your developer, okay,

00:08:42.851 --> 00:08:46.040
whenever you're checking code,
I want you to test your code.

00:08:46.041 --> 00:08:48.580
Maybe have a separate dashboard
that goes, okay, well,

00:08:48.581 --> 00:08:51.389
this was tested in
the back end in Azure.

00:08:51.390 --> 00:08:53.930
And all these parts of the code
were actually tested, and

00:08:53.931 --> 00:08:57.580
the parts of the code that
were not are these edge cases.

00:08:57.581 --> 00:09:00.770
Maybe that would kind of
change how we use verification

00:09:00.771 --> 00:09:01.770
tools, right?

00:09:01.771 --> 00:09:04.180
And okay, I said I was doing
a little bit of science fiction,

00:09:04.181 --> 00:09:07.850
but honestly, what parts of
this are not present yet?

00:09:07.851 --> 00:09:11.010
We can already check all
these assertions, and

00:09:11.011 --> 00:09:14.140
if it is fully verified,
that's the column on the left,

00:09:14.141 --> 00:09:15.280
we can show that, right.

00:09:15.281 --> 00:09:17.990
And then we have, we can
definitely produce warnings.

00:09:17.991 --> 00:09:21.570
We can produce warnings with
different levels of guarantees,

00:09:21.571 --> 00:09:23.064
and we can also rank them.

00:09:23.065 --> 00:09:25.820
We have angelic verification, we
have [INAUDIBLE] specifications.

00:09:25.821 --> 00:09:29.470
And all that allows us to rank
different warnings, right, but

00:09:29.471 --> 00:09:30.630
we don't have the stuff
in the middle.

00:09:30.631 --> 00:09:34.013
We don't really explain what
happened in the cases when we

00:09:34.014 --> 00:09:37.464
don't have a warning, but
we don't have a full proof, and

00:09:37.465 --> 00:09:38.860
that's a lot of cases.

00:09:38.861 --> 00:09:43.536
So maybe if we had that, and
just maybe that might be the one

00:09:43.537 --> 00:09:47.736
element that keeps all of
the rest things together,

00:09:47.737 --> 00:09:50.695
and makes it reasonable
to integrate

00:09:50.696 --> 00:09:55.108
it into a mainstream idea
like Visual Studio, right?

00:09:55.109 --> 00:09:58.555
&gt;&gt; [INAUDIBLE]
&gt;&gt; You can combine differently,

00:09:58.556 --> 00:10:00.939
it could be a combination of
maybe your testing plus whatever

00:10:00.940 --> 00:10:02.790
verification efforts you have.

00:10:02.791 --> 00:10:06.081
But essentially, you would
have an idea of what parts of

00:10:06.082 --> 00:10:08.393
the code were not
actually exercised,

00:10:08.394 --> 00:10:11.702
what parts of the system
behaviors are not checked yet.

00:10:11.703 --> 00:10:16.337
Okay, this was a little bit of
wandering around in my mind, but

00:10:16.338 --> 00:10:20.710
let's talk about how my work at
my PhD fits into what already

00:10:20.711 --> 00:10:24.700
exists, and how is that
a step forward or not.

