more than one iteration,

00:23:33.801 --> 00:23:37.220
then you have to unroll it in
the code and that's not ideal.

00:23:37.221 --> 00:23:39.280
I think that makes it
way less understandable.

00:23:40.440 --> 00:23:43.840
So there's definitely room for
improvement here, right?

00:23:43.841 --> 00:23:46.410
Because you don't know how
relevant these assumption are.

00:23:46.411 --> 00:23:48.880
And then the same thing happens
with conditional launching.

00:23:48.881 --> 00:23:50.720
Let's go back to
what we said before.

00:23:50.721 --> 00:23:51.380
You asked me, well,

00:23:51.381 --> 00:23:54.690
what does it mean that you
have a false state here?

00:23:54.691 --> 00:23:55.518
What it means-

00:23:55.519 --> 00:23:56.686
&gt;&gt; [INAUDIBLE]
&gt;&gt; Sorry, yep.

00:23:56.687 --> 00:24:01.400
&gt;&gt; [INAUDIBLE]
&gt;&gt; No they are, yep.

00:24:01.401 --> 00:24:04.140
So that's why other
studies that they did was,

00:24:04.141 --> 00:24:07.429
basically, checking how
relevant these assumptions,

00:24:07.430 --> 00:24:11.181
these unsound assumptions where
across different [INAUDIBLE].

00:24:11.182 --> 00:24:17.122
Which is part of the sound in
it's manifesto [INAUDIBLE].

00:24:17.123 --> 00:24:18.980
So back to conditional
model checking.

00:24:18.981 --> 00:24:20.360
Well, is this
a perfect solution?

00:24:20.361 --> 00:24:22.610
Well, the thing is, suppose
you expose this to your user?

00:24:22.611 --> 00:24:24.090
Well, first of all, yeah,
it's gonna be huge,

00:24:24.091 --> 00:24:26.720
which is what Tom mentioned.

00:24:26.721 --> 00:24:29.630
But other than that, you see a
false statement and you might be

00:24:29.631 --> 00:24:32.010
wondering, well, how relevant
is that false statement?

00:24:33.200 --> 00:24:34.830
Well, it's not really
relevant because it's

00:24:34.831 --> 00:24:36.600
not reachable there, right?

00:24:36.601 --> 00:24:38.120
So you need to do
something about it in order

00:24:38.121 --> 00:24:40.610
to interpret it.

00:24:41.790 --> 00:24:44.750
So basically this is
where we start from.

00:24:44.751 --> 00:24:47.860
So what is my approach?

00:24:47.861 --> 00:24:49.610
Well, I have two
different approaches.

00:24:49.611 --> 00:24:51.334
One of them is
Execution reports.

00:24:51.335 --> 00:24:54.786
So what we do is we present
a report that captures safety

00:24:54.787 --> 00:24:57.638
assurances in terms of
system executions and

00:24:57.639 --> 00:25:01.970
the relationship to the safety
property of interest, right?

00:25:01.971 --> 00:25:05.592
And then we have another
approach, which is coverage,

00:25:05.593 --> 00:25:07.603
which is sort of The opposite.

00:25:07.604 --> 00:25:10.186
Meanwhile while
execution reports aim for

00:25:10.187 --> 00:25:12.493
detail coverage aims for
[INAUDIBLE].

00:25:12.494 --> 00:25:14.353
And it is an intuitive
definition,

00:25:14.354 --> 00:25:17.639
it's comparable to textbook
coverage which is interesting,

00:25:17.640 --> 00:25:21.860
cuz you get a way to compare two
totally different techniques.

00:25:21.861 --> 00:25:23.790
But they're kind
of complementary.

00:25:24.920 --> 00:25:27.600
So let's start
execution reports.

00:25:27.601 --> 00:25:30.090
So as I mentioned,
what you do is you

00:25:30.091 --> 00:25:32.230
create a report based
on system executions.

00:25:32.231 --> 00:25:35.740
And concretely, what we do
is we provide two sets of

00:25:35.741 --> 00:25:38.330
system executions, one is
the exploration frontier, and

00:25:38.331 --> 00:25:40.440
the other one
are the safe cones.

00:25:40.441 --> 00:25:43.780
Essentially, if you've already
followed the whole part about

00:25:43.781 --> 00:25:46.980
a conditional model checker
what we try to find is paths

00:25:46.981 --> 00:25:50.950
that are feasible, that lead to
the safe the true states and

00:25:50.951 --> 00:25:51.660
the false states.

00:25:52.780 --> 00:25:54.160
And what does it mean for

00:25:54.161 --> 00:25:56.500
an execution to be in
the expression frontier?

00:25:56.501 --> 00:25:58.520
Let's look at
the example on the left.

00:25:58.521 --> 00:26:02.490
We have a verification harness
that exercises the operation,

00:26:02.491 --> 00:26:03.580
two operations of a stack.

00:26:03.581 --> 00:26:05.360
We have push and pop.

00:26:05.361 --> 00:26:06.650
And then it
non-deterministically

00:26:06.651 --> 00:26:07.700
chooses the operation.

00:26:07.701 --> 00:26:08.854
It calls a function and

00:26:08.855 --> 00:26:11.479
then it checks whether
the variant holds, right?

00:26:11.480 --> 00:26:12.810
And does that forever,
basically.

00:26:14.770 --> 00:26:18.490
So what does it mean to have
the execution pushed first in

00:26:18.491 --> 00:26:19.980
the exploration frontier?

00:26:19.981 --> 00:26:22.640
Well, what it means is that
that's the precise point where

00:26:22.641 --> 00:26:24.460
the analysis gave up, right?

00:26:24.461 --> 00:26:27.070
So when it was reaching
the second push call,

00:26:27.071 --> 00:26:30.420
it had to stop for whatever
reason, timeout or anything and

00:26:30.421 --> 00:26:32.370
then it didn't exploit
that system execution, but

00:26:32.371 --> 00:26:35.700
it didn't explore any of
its continuations, right?

00:26:35.701 --> 00:26:37.930
Any possible extension for it.

00:26:39.480 --> 00:26:41.766
And the same holds for
push pop, right?

00:26:41.767 --> 00:26:45.676
So if you had to, you just
finished your model checking

00:26:45.677 --> 00:26:48.570
attempt and you left it for
half an hour.

00:26:48.571 --> 00:26:49.730
Will you be like, okay?

00:26:49.731 --> 00:26:52.050
Ready to go to production,
maybe it wouldn't, right?

00:26:52.051 --> 00:26:55.720
Because you're like, you
didn't check any of the border

00:26:55.721 --> 00:26:58.350
conditions that I might
me interested in, right?

00:26:58.351 --> 00:27:01.620
So this is not great, cuz you
still have to do something.

00:27:01.621 --> 00:27:03.450
You have to test your system or
something.

00:27:03.451 --> 00:27:06.380
But at least you know that your
model checking attempt was

00:27:06.381 --> 00:27:07.990
definitely not good enough.

00:27:07.991 --> 00:27:09.250
And what might happen to you,

00:27:09.251 --> 00:27:13.150
if you're using Bonet model
checking technique is,

00:27:13.151 --> 00:27:15.690
well, you get the idea that,
okay, it didn't present you any

00:27:15.691 --> 00:27:18.230
warnings, but it didn't really
go that far, because whatever it

00:27:18.231 --> 00:27:20.510
couldn't skip some loop or
something like that, anyway.

00:27:20.511 --> 00:27:22.890
&gt;&gt; But don't we run into
the same problem here?

00:27:22.891 --> 00:27:26.113
I mean, the model checker is
gonna grow a lot more, so

00:27:26.114 --> 00:27:29.910
isn't your exploration frontier
and it's going to be huge?

00:27:29.911 --> 00:27:31.970
&gt;&gt; Well,
that's a great question.

00:27:31.971 --> 00:27:33.560
And actually it's not.

00:27:33.561 --> 00:27:37.270
But I mean it depends on what
instances your looking at.

00:27:37.271 --> 00:27:40.970
&gt;&gt; If it's a set of traces,
it seems like it

00:27:40.971 --> 00:27:46.200
could be a very large set and

00:27:46.201 --> 00:27:50.380
many of these traces are sort
of similar to others.

00:27:50.381 --> 00:27:56.090
I mean, you're trying
to sort of summarize

00:27:56.091 --> 00:28:01.200
an exploration which
inherently could be,

00:28:01.201 --> 00:28:05.720
the program besides that and
this could be exponential.

00:28:05.721 --> 00:28:06.360
&gt;&gt; That's true.

00:28:06.361 --> 00:28:07.240
&gt;&gt; Right.
So it seems-

00:28:07.241 --> 00:28:07.780
&gt;&gt; I can talk

00:28:07.781 --> 00:28:08.944
about that a little bit. Yeah.

00:28:08.945 --> 00:28:10.281
&gt;&gt; Okay.
[INAUDIBLE]

00:28:10.282 --> 00:28:11.141
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I had

00:28:11.142 --> 00:28:12.791
the same question, I mean,

00:28:12.792 --> 00:28:16.460
I guess with model checking the
underlying assumption is that

00:28:16.461 --> 00:28:19.540
with even a small like if you're
concurrency or something like.

00:28:19.541 --> 00:28:21.310
Even with a small number
of threads you're

00:28:21.311 --> 00:28:22.488
going to find bugs.

00:28:22.489 --> 00:28:26.214
You're not gonna find the bugs
when there's 16 threads,

00:28:26.215 --> 00:28:27.902
because it's way too far.

00:28:27.903 --> 00:28:30.581
But the whole point
of stopping is,

00:28:30.582 --> 00:28:35.149
if you keep going your state
gets bigger and bigger, right?

00:28:35.150 --> 00:28:38.177
And so it's seem that this
frontier you're talking about

00:28:38.178 --> 00:28:41.266
will just get exponentially
larger, like Tom was saying,

00:28:41.267 --> 00:28:44.170
what would a person do
with that at that point?

00:28:44.171 --> 00:28:46.320
&gt;&gt; That's kinda true, let me
tell you about that first, and

00:28:46.321 --> 00:28:48.540
then I'll tell you about
Tom's observation.

00:28:48.541 --> 00:28:51.050
So first of all, the thing is,
with branching, is you that you

00:28:51.051 --> 00:28:53.200
have that assumption, but that's
a very general assumption.

00:28:53.201 --> 00:28:55.200
So one of the things that
inspirited me, actually,

00:28:55.201 --> 00:28:58.440
to start doing all this, is that
I did an internship here, and

00:28:58.441 --> 00:28:59.900
I was using Corral.

00:28:59.901 --> 00:29:02.210
A lot of those things that I
kept doing all the time was

00:29:02.211 --> 00:29:05.020
adding false assertions
to know how far my

00:29:05.021 --> 00:29:06.200
explosion attempt went.

00:29:06.201 --> 00:29:09.270
And I was like, yeah,
it's true that, yeah,

00:29:09.271 --> 00:29:10.310
shell of bugs and all that.

00:29:10.311 --> 00:29:11.510
But how far did it go?

00:29:11.511 --> 00:29:14.000
Did I get after
the initialization look?

00:29:14.001 --> 00:29:17.010
&gt;&gt; So basically can you prove
this a certain false for me?

00:29:17.011 --> 00:29:19.180
You just sprinkle
the assert false?

00:29:19.181 --> 00:29:22.261
&gt;&gt; I would sprinkle assert false
after certain loops, cuz I mean,

00:29:22.262 --> 00:29:24.269
I know how many
iterations this can have.

00:29:24.270 --> 00:29:25.517
&gt;&gt; But can you even get here?

00:29:25.518 --> 00:29:26.371
&gt;&gt; Can I even get here?

00:29:26.372 --> 00:29:31.275
&gt;&gt; Okay, sure [CROSSTALK]
&gt;&gt; It's hard to know

00:29:31.276 --> 00:29:31.917
what happens.

00:29:31.918 --> 00:29:34.223
&gt;&gt; It's a smoke test,
but I mean,

00:29:34.224 --> 00:29:37.043
I'm not the type of
super novice user,

00:29:37.044 --> 00:29:41.145
still I had to do some really
kind of dark stuff to make sure

00:29:41.146 --> 00:29:45.610
that the model checker was doing
some of what I wanted it to do.

00:29:45.611 --> 00:29:49.060
It's true, it's true,
but we need

00:29:49.061 --> 00:29:51.396
at least a tiny window into
what's going on inside that.

00:29:51.397 --> 00:29:54.990
&gt;&gt; A model checker can at least
do statement code for you,

00:29:54.991 --> 00:29:57.550
because when it explores
a statement at least it reached.

00:29:57.551 --> 00:29:59.520
You can say just like a test,

00:29:59.521 --> 00:30:02.352
because it's fundamentally
executing the code.

00:30:02.353 --> 00:30:06.180
&gt;&gt; We're gonna talk about
coverage in a couple of slides.

00:30:06.181 --> 00:30:07.030
&gt;&gt; So the frontier so

00:30:07.031 --> 00:30:10.345
the way you define the frontier
is a set of traces, right?

00:30:10.346 --> 00:30:11.800
&gt;&gt; A set of traces,
that's right.

00:30:11.801 --> 00:30:14.450
And your question was
actually very good.

00:30:14.451 --> 00:30:17.140
So you might have an explanation
number of traces.

00:30:17.141 --> 00:30:20.412
That's true, one of the things
is we don't say that we're gonna

00:30:20.413 --> 00:30:22.090
present the whole set of traces.

00:30:22.091 --> 00:30:24.060
We say that we're
gonna present some.

00:30:24.061 --> 00:30:25.140
And why is that important?

00:30:25.141 --> 00:30:25.690
Well, first of all,

00:30:25.691 --> 00:30:28.760
because if you see one
trace that is too short you

00:30:28.761 --> 00:30:31.935
might already realize that your
expression is not deep enough.

00:30:31.936 --> 00:30:32.770
That's one of the things.

00:30:32.771 --> 00:30:34.330
And then your other
question was, well,

00:30:34.331 --> 00:30:36.480
you might have a ton of
traces that are very similar,

00:30:36.481 --> 00:30:37.280
why is that?

00:30:37.281 --> 00:30:38.480
Well, because inside the push,

00:30:38.481 --> 00:30:42.020
you might have an if to whatever
condition happens then and

00:30:42.021 --> 00:30:44.550
you have one path that goes
on the true condition, and

00:30:44.551 --> 00:30:48.400
the other path it goes on the
false condition, so that's true.

00:30:48.401 --> 00:30:49.280
And that's a problem that we

00:30:49.281 --> 00:30:51.590
actually don't tackle
explicitly in our paper.

00:30:51.591 --> 00:30:54.470
I did tackle in order to
look at the output and

00:30:54.471 --> 00:30:56.470
what I do is basically
I group those traces.

00:30:56.471 --> 00:30:58.410
What I look at is, for instance,

00:30:58.411 --> 00:31:00.960
in this case API operations,
right?

00:31:00.961 --> 00:31:06.750
So it's true that I don't
get the full detail,

00:31:06.751 --> 00:31:10.120
but if I know that I didn't do
more than two loop iterations

00:31:10.121 --> 00:31:12.700
of the main loop,
we would probably agree that

00:31:12.701 --> 00:31:14.450
that's not a very
deep exploration.

00:31:14.451 --> 00:31:17.040
Well, for none of
the examples that I checked

00:31:17.041 --> 00:31:20.112
in the SV Comp benchmark,

00:31:20.113 --> 00:31:23.730
I had never reached this third
iteration of loop, right?

00:31:23.731 --> 00:31:26.050
So it does actually happen that
you don't really get that far.

00:31:26.051 --> 00:31:28.598
And it really depends on how
non-deterministic your instance

00:31:28.599 --> 00:31:29.277
is and so forth.

00:31:29.278 --> 00:31:32.691
But it's not entirely crazy to
have these sort of instances.

00:31:32.692 --> 00:31:36.117
&gt;&gt; I guess that you're
saying that these traces,

00:31:36.118 --> 00:31:39.287
even if they are abstract
where [INAUDIBLE],

00:31:39.288 --> 00:31:42.390
they cover the actual
ART when you hear it.

00:31:42.391 --> 00:31:44.390
They might switch
traces of there.

00:31:44.391 --> 00:31:45.310
&gt;&gt; That's right, yeah.

00:31:45.311 --> 00:31:46.900
&gt;&gt; Well, that's okay if
you still know that you

00:31:46.901 --> 00:31:48.950
they will cover your two pushes.

00:31:48.951 --> 00:31:49.670
&gt;&gt; That's right, yeah.

00:31:49.671 --> 00:31:52.420
&gt;&gt; And that's thing is?

00:31:52.421 --> 00:31:54.770
&gt;&gt; Yeah, that's the sort of
insight that we wanna get.

00:31:54.771 --> 00:31:58.720
Then we have safe cones and
what are safe cones?

00:31:58.721 --> 00:32:01.880
Well, this is essentially
paths to the collapsed part of

00:32:01.881 --> 00:32:04.860
the assumption right, so
this is the true nodes.

00:32:04.861 --> 00:32:06.450
It's those places where
you managed to prove that

00:32:06.451 --> 00:32:07.770
something was safe.

00:32:07.771 --> 00:32:09.730
And let's see it with
an example, again, so

00:32:09.731 --> 00:32:12.324
we have a verification
harness on the left.

00:32:12.325 --> 00:32:14.950
That non-deterministically
chooses the mode of operation of

00:32:14.951 --> 00:32:17.250
a system, right, and
we have mode one and mode two.

00:32:17.251 --> 00:32:20.060
And our original verification
result be unknown,

00:32:20.061 --> 00:32:21.330
table limit exceeded or
something like that.

00:32:21.331 --> 00:32:25.860
What we get now is, well, the
system execution that only calls

00:32:25.861 --> 00:32:28.790
mode one is the safe trace,
what does that mean?

00:32:28.791 --> 00:32:32.350
Well, it means that your system
is safe to use under mode one,

00:32:32.351 --> 00:32:35.320
because any extension of
it will also be safe,

00:32:35.321 --> 00:32:37.210
that means there's no
assertion failure.

00:32:37.211 --> 00:32:38.380
That's a very strong statement.

00:32:38.381 --> 00:32:41.780
That's much better than what
you would get with testing.

00:32:41.781 --> 00:32:43.950
And much better than what you
would get with an unknown

00:32:43.951 --> 00:32:45.650
result, right?

00:32:45.651 --> 00:32:48.400
But it's also detailed
enough to tell you that,

00:32:48.401 --> 00:32:52.536
well, even though mode one has
been very successfully proved

00:32:52.537 --> 00:32:56.060
safe than mode two is not so
good, right?

00:32:56.061 --> 00:32:58.670
Because we get pretty much the
same case that we got before.

00:32:58.671 --> 00:33:01.510
We didn't get past the situation
of the while loops, so

00:33:01.511 --> 00:33:03.150
it gives you some insight into,
well,

00:33:03.151 --> 00:33:07.050
maybe you can remove mode one
from your verification harness.

00:33:07.051 --> 00:33:08.950
And then maybe you can pay
attention to mode two and

00:33:08.951 --> 00:33:10.710
probably use another technique
because you're not making

00:33:10.711 --> 00:33:12.410
progress with this, right?

00:33:12.411 --> 00:33:14.713
This is all very manual,
though, I do admit that,

00:33:14.714 --> 00:33:17.725
it's not like we're gonna get a
tool working right away, sorry.

00:33:17.726 --> 00:33:19.759
&gt;&gt; [INAUDIBLE] I think
[INAUDIBLE] give an under

00:33:19.760 --> 00:33:23.290
approximation instead of being
definitely sure about it, right?

00:33:23.291 --> 00:33:24.588
&gt;&gt; That's right, yeah. So.

00:33:24.589 --> 00:33:24.725
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:33:24.725 --> 00:33:29.230
So, we're not gonna go over each
of these formulas right now,

00:33:29.231 --> 00:33:33.226
but what I wanna emphasize is
that part of the work is that

00:33:33.227 --> 00:33:35.863
we formalize what
the guarantees for

00:33:35.864 --> 00:33:38.600
each of these parts
of the output are.

00:33:38.601 --> 00:33:41.320
And what are the connections
between our output and

00:33:41.321 --> 00:33:43.830
the safety guarantees because
I think that's relevant for

00:33:43.831 --> 00:33:45.630
making it intuitive and
understandable.

00:33:45.631 --> 00:33:48.500
But what we are gonna do is
go over these formulas and

00:33:48.501 --> 00:33:50.710
how they apply to
the previous examples, right?

00:33:50.711 --> 00:33:53.320
So, we have the first
example in which we saw

00:33:53.321 --> 00:33:54.480
the exploration frontier.

00:33:54.481 --> 00:33:55.870
And what does it mean
that we have push,

00:33:55.871 --> 00:33:57.770
push in the exploration
frontier?

00:33:57.771 --> 00:33:59.810
Well, it means, A,
that it's feasible, right?

00:33:59.811 --> 00:34:00.940
It's an actual system execution.

00:34:00.941 --> 00:34:02.050
You don't have to go checking,

00:34:02.051 --> 00:34:06.025
well, can I actually ever
do two pushes in a row?

00:34:06.026 --> 00:34:07.200
No, we actually checked that for

00:34:07.201 --> 00:34:09.370
you because that's
a reasonable thing to do.

00:34:10.530 --> 00:34:13.240
But then we also know that
whatever safety property

00:34:13.241 --> 00:34:15.290
we were checking for, holes,

00:34:15.291 --> 00:34:18.020
right that means that
your analysis is sound.

00:34:18.021 --> 00:34:21.070
If there a bug in that trace
we would have found it.

00:34:21.071 --> 00:34:23.740
And then you know basically the
same thing that you would know

00:34:23.741 --> 00:34:24.710
for a test case.

00:34:24.711 --> 00:34:27.550
Okay, it's right if you see
a test case that passes you now

00:34:27.551 --> 00:34:31.990
very well that didn't fail which
is kind of intuitive, but still.

00:34:31.991 --> 00:34:34.130
And then you get that
